"Compares response profiles across factor combinations to reveal whether one factor’s effect depends on another.\n\nUse when: An experiment or stratified comparison has two categorical factors. Readers need to inspect differences in effects across levels of the second factor.\n\nAvoid when: Cell sizes are too small or empty to support the displayed means without clear qualification.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes one factor’s ordered levels. Vertical position encodes a response summary; separate lines identify levels of the other factor.\n\nBuild: Calculate a named response summary and sample size for each factor combination. Choose a stable factor-level order and draw one profile per second-factor level. Add uncertainty intervals where available and label profiles outside the data region.\n\nCheck: Check every combination uses the same response units and statistic. Verify missing combinations remain visible as missing. Inspect sample balance and any adjustment method used for means.\n\nPitfalls: Nonparallel profiles suggest interaction on the displayed response scale, not automatic statistical significance. Crossings may be meaningful; use separate panels when labels or paths cannot be followed clearly.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic material strength across two temperatures for three coatings, retaining cell counts and uncertainty intervals.\n\nAlternatives: Main effects plot, Grouped bar chart, Block plot."
